{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-162959",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{121{t}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{36}}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}}=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{121{t}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{(11t)^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{|11t|} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{|11|\\cdot |t|} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{11\\cdot |t|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $t<0,$ so we have $|t|=-t.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{11\\cdot |t|} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{11\\cdot (-t)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{6}{11t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
